Tests are written in the Ruby programming language using the
Test::Unit framework. The Parslet library is used to parse responses
from the ToastStunt server under test.

To run the tests:

1) Ensure you have Ruby 1.9.3 or later installed.

2) Ensure you have Bundler installed.

3) Install dependencies.
    bundle install

4) Create the following symlinks in the executables/ directory:
    ln -s `which echo` echo
    ln -s `which sleep` sleep
    ln -s `which true` true

5) Build the server and copy the executable into the test directory.

6) Verify the settings in test.yml are accurate for your configuration.

7) In one console/terminal/window, run the ToastStunt server in the test directory:
    ./moo Test.db /dev/null 9898

8) In another console/terminal/window, run the tests:
    make

9) Clean up the moo executable and the files created in /tmp:
    make clean

Native PostgreSQL ownership regressions can run independently of the MOO test
server. They require PostgreSQL server tools (located through pg_config), Python
3, GNU objcopy, and a CMake Unix Makefiles LeakCheck build with PostgreSQL support.
Run as a regular user (PostgreSQL initdb refuses root):

    cmake -S . -B /tmp/toaststunt-sql-tests -G 'Unix Makefiles' -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=LeakCheck -DCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON
    cmake --build /tmp/toaststunt-sql-tests -j2
    python3 test/run_sql_ownership.py /tmp/toaststunt-sql-tests

The runner reuses the freshly built server objects, starts a private temporary
PostgreSQL instance with TCP disabled, and stops/removes it afterward. It checks
successful rows, statement errors, deferred-constraint COMMIT failures, untouched
output on failure, recovery, pool expiration and shutdown. Each case repeats five
times and runs garbage collection followed by an explicit LeakSanitizer check;
AddressSanitizer also detects invalid deletion through the SQLSession base class.
Rebuild before rerunning after any source change.
